{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/296102-considering-alternatives-to-the-sothic-cycle","id":296102,"title":"considering alternatives to the Sothic Cycle","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-29T13:54:51-07:00","id":296102,"title":"considering alternatives to the Sothic Cycle","content":"Greetings all,<br /> <br /> I've been fascinated with the odd style of storytelling that <i>Mummy</i> gives since it was released, and in the last few months I began brewing up a chronicle idea to start next year, so I've been reading up on <i>Mummy</i>, and there's a few things bother me / are still unclear; I'll probably ask them in different threads as they come up. Right now, though, the Sothic Cycle is kind of bothering me, and I was wondering if anyone else had used different chronologies - or if anyone has compelling reasons why to keep it.<br /> <br /> My concern comes from researching astrological ages outside of nWoD's context, so there's an obvious wall where you can say &quot;this is what the game decided to go with, and it works for the presentation they wanted&quot;. I'm looking beyond that wall, thinking of possibly re-configuring things to a different cosmology. The reason for it is that I specifically started researching Chinese astrology and other conceptions of astrological ages, trying to find ties to the Sothic Cycle - and in so doing looked at the ways they were calculated. And from that research, I found the Sothic Cycle to be pretty unconvincing.<br /> <br /> From what I can tell, it was calculated using the rising of Sirius - which happened every 365.25 days - compared to the solar calendar Egyptians were using of 360 days: the 1461 year cycle was how long it took for the calendars to match up again. Which is to say that this cycle comes from a mistake in the math / trying to line up two different calculations of a single year. As far as cosmic cycles and significant events go, that doesn't seem like a very compelling one to me. I haven't been able to find archives of the original developer blogs explaining Mummy's Sothic cycle, so I don't know the writers' reasons for using it, apart from the Egyptian connection and/or convenience of the timetable (4 cycles lining up to 2012). Does anyone know why this cycle was used, or underlying mystical connections that I missed? I know Sirius had a lot of religious significance in various cultures, and was associated with Isis (I think) in Egypt, but the cycle itself...<br /> <br /> Usually astrological ages are calculated based on the Earth's wobbling rotational axis (tugged by the sun and moon), in which the axis moves in a full circle, changing our view of the sun relative to the constellations. This rotation is 1 degree every 72 years, leading to a full cycle of 25,920 years. Normally this is lined up to western constellations (leading to 12 &quot;months&quot; of about 2160 years, lining up with the astrological symbols - but the difference in size of the constellations leads to debate). This calculation of the Great Year came to us from the Greeks - but my <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Year\" target=\"_blank\">wikipedia-crawling</a> suggested they learned it from Egypt. <br /> I find that to be a more convincing mystical cycle, personally. I would want to divorce it from western astronomy and bring it more in line with Egyptian culture - and <i>Mummy</i>'s cosmology in particular. I'd probably favoring dividing up to have slightly shorter cycles for the mummies (and more standardized ones) for storytelling reasons. I might also look at overlaying the Great Year with the solar (possibly aligning to Sirius instead of Sol - but they're virtually identical) and lunar calendars.<br /> <br /> <br /> I'm curious if anyone else has tried this, or has any interesting ideas for it, or good reasons not to. <i>Mummy</i> presents a pretty good chronology that works well for its story; I'm just the kind of person that looks for cosmological reasoning and cross-cultural ties, and gets hung up on this sort of thing. I would spare myself the challenging research and system hacking and just go with the corebook, but there's part of me that really wants a compelling mystical reason to sink my teeth into and anchor the magic.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?<br /> ~Seraph Kitty","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-30T01:05:16-07:00","id":296746,"title":"","content":"Snipped from the story hook thread (Apologies for quoting myself):<br /> <br /> .) The scrolls of the Sesha-Hebsu explain that the metaphysical properties of the Sothic Wheel have been incorporated into the Rite of the Return affecting all Arisen, allowing them to reverse their natural state, that of being dead, every 1400-something years.<br /> <br /> Recent findings suggest the existence of a 260-day Sacred Round cycle, whose properties might in some way correspond to specific complexities the Su-Menent remember about the process of the Rite of Return. It seems unlikely but plausible that the Arisen might, given enough time and the discovery and correct use of very powerful relics, commit the heresy of artificially simulating a locally generated Sothic Turn that will cause all Arisen of an entire continent to come back to life prematurely .. repeatedly every 260 days.<br /> <br /> The Su-Menent, loyal to the Judges, don't like the theological implications of these findings. Still, to many of the Arisen, the prospect of being capable of returning to life in that frequency and without the constraints of a First Purpose, is a very attractive one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-30T02:54:37-07:00","id":296809,"title":"","content":"The Sothic cycle that Mummies awaken to aren't perfectly aligned anyway. Mummies were created over a hundred year period, and the magic isn't perfect nor does it seem to be intentional - Mummies are known to awaken a couple decades earlier or later than they might have predicted.<br /> <br /> So a rarer Mummy might wake up in the early 1930s or until the late 2000s, right? Geez maybe my math is off a little, too","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-30T03:17:20-07:00","id":296837,"title":"","content":"Warning: spoilers and speculation ahead, that my mobile device prevents quarantining in the usual spoilery fashion. So.. The Deceived work with Stars. Everyone who knows the spoilers knows that the Deceived work with stars and patterns. Also, everyone knows all SIX guilds had to participate in the Rite of Return (ask the Muse hus opinion of the Philosopher). All six guilds performed the Rite. But one guild.. Knew what was going on (again, ask the Muse). prices had to be paid and compromises had to be made. What if the idea of sothic risings, sothic turns, the idea mummies can rise for &quot;vacation time&quot; is something the Traitors snuck into the Rite? they know Seba.. They know willworking and fate, and kn ow that ma'at is a price to be paid. What if the Lost Guild, knowing they were needed for the Rite, worked in their own loopholes? namely, sothic rising, utterances, sadikh, and apotheiosis? what if the deceived made sure part of the Rite was their own, allowing Mummies for more freedom than the Senate designed (save, perhaps, the TemAkh)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":935,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","user":"Cleverest of Things","date":".  grammar 10-30-2014, 06:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-30T04:05:44-07:00","id":296880,"title":"","content":"I didn't mean to suggest I had an issue with how long there was between risings, or that there's something fishy about the main concept. I like the idea of these rising, and the timing of them seems fine enough to me. I'd just like to see them connected to something with occult meaning.<br /> <br /> Mainly what's bugging me is why the sothic cycle in particular was chosen (from a perspective outside the game)? At least from my understanding of <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sothic_cycle\" target=\"_blank\">what they are</a>, the cycle comes from aligning two year-long calendars that are slightly off - there doesn't seem to be a relation to any cosmic process or star pattern that relates to sothic cycles; even though its based on the difference between Sirius and Sol, that difference is actually nominal, and more attributable to a .25 day error on the part of the Egyptian solar calendar.<br /> <br /> Now, I could be totally misinterpreting something about how Sothic cycles are calculated which actually <i>does</i> point to an astronomical significance, but unless I did, there are some other cosmic patterns which I think would make a more compelling basis an long-term cycle of lifeforce.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-30T05:11:49-07:00","id":296968,"title":"","content":"I guess what I'm saying is that I wouldn't worry about the exact mathematics. It's mostly a thematic thing with liberal use of a real world concept.<br /> <br /> And to Cleverest of Things, that's an interesting theory but despite disagreeing with it my biggest thing is that I never considered sadikh to be among the loopholes in the Rite. Memory is, and even Pillars on some level since you need to reclaim there for Apotheosis","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}